Step A: Convert Components to Context Consumption
Order of Conversion (Safe & Logical)

Dashboard (read-only, low risk)

AcuteTracker (writes to medical history)

DoseCalculator (needs latest weight)

AppointmentManager (multi-domain data)

Remaining components as needed

You can stop at any point and the app will still work.

1️⃣ Dashboard (FIRST – simplest)
BEFORE (current pattern)
<Dashboard
  onNavigate={setActiveView}
  latestWeight={latestWeight}
  childProfile={childProfile}
/>

AFTER (context-driven)
Dashboard.tsx
import { useNavigation } from "../context/NavigationContext";
import { useChildProfile } from "../context/ChildProfileContext";
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const Dashboard: React.FC = () => {
  const { setActiveView } = useNavigation();
  const { childProfile } = useChildProfile();
  const { latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Existing UI unchanged */}
    </div>
  );
};


✅ Remove props
✅ No behavioural change
✅ Safe refactor

2️⃣ AcuteTracker (Writes clinical data)
BEFORE
<AcuteTracker
  onLogEntry={handleAddAcuteLog}
  entries={medicalHistory.acuteLogs}
  childWeight={latestWeight}
/>

AFTER
AcuteTracker.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const AcuteTracker: React.FC = () => {
  const { history, addAcuteLog, latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Replace props with context */}
      {/* entries → history.acuteLogs */}
      {/* onLogEntry → addAcuteLog */}
      {/* childWeight → latestWeight */}
    </div>
  );
};


🔒 This guarantees:

Logs are always persisted

IDs are generated safely

No accidental overwrites

3️⃣ DoseCalculator (Clinically sensitive)
BEFORE
<DoseCalculator initialWeight={latestWeight} />

AFTER
DoseCalculator.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const DoseCalculator: React.FC = () => {
  const { latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  if (!latestWeight) {
    return (
      <div className="p-6 bg-yellow-50 rounded-xl">
        <p className="font-semibold">Weight required</p>
        <p className="text-sm">
          Please record a recent weight before calculating doses.
        </p>
      </div>
    );
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Use latestWeight safely */}
    </div>
  );
};


🩺 This is clinically safer than passing a possibly stale prop.

4️⃣ AppointmentManager (Multi-domain)
BEFORE
<AppointmentManager
  history={medicalHistory}
  onAddAppointment={handleAddAppointment}
  onUpdateAppointments={...}
/>

AFTER
AppointmentManager.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const AppointmentManager: React.FC = () => {
  const { history, updateHistory } = useMedicalHistory();

  const addAppointment = (apt) => {
    updateHistory({
      appointments: [...history.appointments, apt]
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {/* appointments → history.appointments */}
    </div>
  );
};


You can later re-introduce smarter logic (e.g. auto-adding specialists) inside the context instead of here.

5️⃣ Clean AppContent Rendering

Now AppContent becomes a pure view switcher:

const views: Record<ViewType, JSX.Element> = {
  dashboard: <Dashboard />,
  acutelogs: <AcuteTracker />,
  dosage: <DoseCalculator />,
  appointments: <AppointmentManager />,
  // etc
};

✅ How You Know You Did This Right

App.tsx has no medical logic

Components import contexts directly

No more deep prop chains

Medical writes go through one place

This is exactly how real clinical apps are structured.

