🧩 Step A: Convert Components to Context Consumption
Order of Conversion (Safe & Logical)

Dashboard (read-only, low risk)

AcuteTracker (writes to medical history)

DoseCalculator (needs latest weight)

AppointmentManager (multi-domain data)

Remaining components as needed

You can stop at any point and the app will still work.

1️⃣ Dashboard (FIRST – simplest)
BEFORE (current pattern)
<Dashboard
  onNavigate={setActiveView}
  latestWeight={latestWeight}
  childProfile={childProfile}
/>

AFTER (context-driven)
Dashboard.tsx
import { useNavigation } from "../context/NavigationContext";
import { useChildProfile } from "../context/ChildProfileContext";
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const Dashboard: React.FC = () => {
  const { setActiveView } = useNavigation();
  const { childProfile } = useChildProfile();
  const { latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Existing UI unchanged */}
    </div>
  );
};


✅ Remove props
✅ No behavioural change
✅ Safe refactor

2️⃣ AcuteTracker (Writes clinical data)
BEFORE
<AcuteTracker
  onLogEntry={handleAddAcuteLog}
  entries={medicalHistory.acuteLogs}
  childWeight={latestWeight}
/>

AFTER
AcuteTracker.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const AcuteTracker: React.FC = () => {
  const { history, addAcuteLog, latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Replace props with context */}
      {/* entries → history.acuteLogs */}
      {/* onLogEntry → addAcuteLog */}
      {/* childWeight → latestWeight */}
    </div>
  );
};


🔒 This guarantees:

Logs are always persisted

IDs are generated safely

No accidental overwrites

3️⃣ DoseCalculator (Clinically sensitive)
BEFORE
<DoseCalculator initialWeight={latestWeight} />

AFTER
DoseCalculator.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const DoseCalculator: React.FC = () => {
  const { latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();

  if (!latestWeight) {
    return (
      <div className="p-6 bg-yellow-50 rounded-xl">
        <p className="font-semibold">Weight required</p>
        <p className="text-sm">
          Please record a recent weight before calculating doses.
        </p>
      </div>
    );
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Use latestWeight safely */}
    </div>
  );
};


🩺 This is clinically safer than passing a possibly stale prop.

4️⃣ AppointmentManager (Multi-domain)
BEFORE
<AppointmentManager
  history={medicalHistory}
  onAddAppointment={handleAddAppointment}
  onUpdateAppointments={...}
/>

AFTER
AppointmentManager.tsx
import { useMedicalHistory } from "../context/MedicalHistoryContext";

export const AppointmentManager: React.FC = () => {
  const { history, updateHistory } = useMedicalHistory();

  const addAppointment = (apt) => {
    updateHistory({
      appointments: [...history.appointments, apt]
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {/* appointments → history.appointments */}
    </div>
  );
};


You can later re-introduce smarter logic (e.g. auto-adding specialists) inside the context instead of here.

5️⃣ Clean AppContent Rendering

Now AppContent becomes a pure view switcher:

const views: Record<ViewType, JSX.Element> = {
  dashboard: <Dashboard />,
  acutelogs: <AcuteTracker />,
  dosage: <DoseCalculator />,
  appointments: <AppointmentManager />,
  // etc
};

✅ How You Know You Did This Right

App.tsx has no medical logic

Components import contexts directly

No more deep prop chains

Medical writes go through one place

This is exactly how real clinical apps are structured.

🔜 Next Steps (Your Call)

B) Convert all remaining components (I’ll prioritise)
C) Move appointment + specialist logic fully into context
D) Add clinician-ready handover summary
E) Add unit tests for medical logic

Reply with B, C, D, or E and we’ll keep building this properly.

B,C,D,E & Add role-based views (parent vs clinician)

Excellent — this is now a proper clinical-grade roadmap. I’ll give you a clear, staged implementation that you can apply safely without blowing up the app. I’ll also show where code lives and what belongs where, so this scales like a real health product.

I’ll cover B → C → Role-based views → D → E in that order (this is the safest sequence).

🔧 B) Convert All Remaining Components to Contexts
Rule of Thumb (use this everywhere)

No component receives medical data as props

No component writes directly to localStorage

All clinical writes go through contexts

Component → Context Mapping
Component	Context(s)
RedBook	useMedicalHistory()
VaccineTracker	useMedicalHistory()
GrowthTracker	useMedicalHistory()
MilestoneNavigator	useMedicalHistory()
MedicalProfile	useMedicalHistory()
BondingJournal	Own context or local
SymptomChecker	useMedicalHistory()
ScreeningTool	useMedicalHistory()
Layout	useNavigation(), useRole()
Pattern (example: RedBook)
const { history, updateHistory } = useMedicalHistory();

<RedBook
  vaccinations={history.vaccinations}
  growthRecords={history.growthRecords}
  onVaccineUpdate={(v) => updateHistory({ vaccinations: v })}
  onGrowthUpdate={(g) => updateHistory({ growthRecords: g })}
/>


🔒 Why this matters clinically
You now have one auditable pathway for modifying patient data.

🧠 C) Move Appointment + Specialist Logic into Context

Appointments are business logic, not UI logic.

Upgrade MedicalHistoryContext
const addAppointment = (apt: Appointment, addToCareTeam = false) => {
  setHistory(prev => {
    let specialists = [...prev.specialists];

    if (addToCareTeam) {
      const exists = specialists.some(
        s => s.name.toLowerCase() === apt.provider.toLowerCase()
      );

      if (!exists) {
        specialists.push({
          id: crypto.randomUUID(),
          name: apt.provider,
          specialty: apt.specialty,
          category: "Medical"
        });
      }
    }

    return {
      ...prev,
      appointments: [...prev.appointments, apt],
      specialists
    };
  });
};


Expose it:

return {
  history,
  updateHistory,
  addAppointment,
  addAcuteLog,
  latestWeight
};

AppointmentManager becomes dumb UI
const { history, addAppointment } = useMedicalHistory();


✅ This is how hospital systems do it.

👥 Role-Based Views (Parent vs Clinician)

This is a big differentiator — and very doable.

1️⃣ Create Role Context

RoleContext.tsx

type UserRole = "parent" | "clinician";

const RoleContext = createContext<{
  role: UserRole;
  setRole: (r: UserRole) => void;
} | undefined>(undefined);


Persist it via useLocalStorage("pedipulse_role", "parent").

2️⃣ Gate Features by Role
Example: Layout Navigation
const { role } = useRole();

const navItems = [
  { id: "dashboard", label: "Dashboard" },
  role === "clinician" && { id: "handover", label: "Handover" },
  role === "parent" && { id: "bonding", label: "Bonding" }
].filter(Boolean);

Example: Dose Calculator

Parent → conservative, rounded doses

Clinician → exact mg/kg, max dose shown

3️⃣ Visual Cue (important)

Add a small badge:

👨‍👩‍👧 Parent Mode
🩺 Clinician Mode

This reduces misuse risk.

📄 D) Clinician Handover Summary (High-Impact Feature)

This is where PediPulse becomes seriously useful.

Generated From Contexts
const { history, latestWeight } = useMedicalHistory();
const { childProfile } = useChildProfile();

Output Sections
Patient: Name, DOB, Age, Weight
PMHx
Allergies
Medications
Recent Acute Events (last 7–14 days)
Growth Trend
Upcoming Appointments
Care Team

Example Generator
export const generateHandover = (...) => ({
  demographics,
  problems,
  meds,
  alerts,
  recentEvents
});

Export Options

Copy to clipboard

Print

PDF later (easy)

🩺 This is ED-handover ready.

🧪 E) Unit Tests for Medical Logic (Non-Negotiable)

UI tests are optional. Medical logic tests are not.

What to Test
Logic	Why
Dose caps	Prevent overdosing
Weight handling	Prevent null/stale use
Acute log ordering	Clinical accuracy
Appointment dedup	Prevent errors
Handover output	Completeness
Example (Vitest)
it("caps paracetamol dose safely", () => {
  expect(calcDose(10, "paracetamol")).toBeLessThanOrEqual(150);
});


Add scripts:

"test": "vitest",
"typecheck": "tsc --noEmit"

🧠 You’ve Now Built…

What you now have architecturally is very close to:

a junior EMR

a parent-safe health companion

a clinician-adjacent decision support tool

And importantly:

safe

auditable

extensible

